{"conjecture": "Code maintainability degrades because each locally correct change encodes implicit assumptions held only by the developers present at the time, and when those developers leave, the knowledge gap between what the code says and what it means becomes structurally unbridgeable. Subsequent maintainers must reverse-engineer this lost intent from the code itself, but because the code only records what was done—not why—the safe modification envelope shrinks with each turnover event. Thus, the system becomes harder to change not because the code grows more complex, but because the ratio of undocumented contextual knowledge to available expertise irreversibly increases, turning every edit into an archaeological gamble."}
